one hidden layer.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.080
In this video, I'm going to just give you the equations you need to implement in order

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.640
to get backpropagation or to get gradient descent working.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.180
And then in the video after this one, I'll give some more intuition about why these

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:26.040
particular equations are the accurate equations or the correct equations for computing the

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.900
gradients you need for your neural network.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:37.380
So your neural network with a single hidden layer for now will have parameters W1, B1,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.500
W2 and B2.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:50.140
And so as a reminder, if you have an x or alternatively, N0 input features and N1

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:56.380
hidden units and N2 output units.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.940
In our example, so far we've only had N2 equals 1.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.380
Then the matrix W1 will be N1 by N0.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.780
B1 will be an N1 dimensional vector.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:14.260
So you can write that as an N1 by 1 dimensional matrix, really a column vector.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:25.940
The dimensions of W2 will be N2 by N1 and the dimension of B2 will be N2 by 1.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.380
Where again, so far we've only seen examples where N2 is equal to 1 where you have just

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.940
one single hidden unit.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:39.380
So you also have a cost function for your neural network.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.820
And for now, I'm just going to assume that you're doing binary classification.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:49.820
So in that case, the cost of your parameters as follows.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:56.620
It's going to be 1 over m of the average of that loss function.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:02.580
And so L here is the loss when your neural network predicts y hat, right?

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.420
This is really a 2 when the ground truth label is equal to y.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.460
And if you're doing binary classification, the loss function can be exactly what you

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.580
use for logistic progression earlier.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:20.460
So to train the parameters of your algorithm, you need to perform gradient descent.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.940
When training a neural network, it's important to initialize the parameters

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.780
randomly, routed into all zeros.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.380
We'll see later why that's the case.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:32.140
But after initializing the parameters to something, each loop of gradient

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.020
descent would compute the predictions.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:42.060
So you basically compute y hat i for i equals 1 through m, say.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.460
And then you need to compute the derivatives.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.540
So you need to compute dw1.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.060
And that's basically the derivative of the cost function with respect to the

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.340
parameter w1.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.700
You need to compute another variable, which we're going to call db1,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.380
which is the derivative or the slope of the cost function with respect to the

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.140
variable b1, and so on.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:09.740
Similarly for the other parameters w2 and b2.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:16.980
And then finally, the gradient descent update would be to update w1 as w1 minus

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.580
alpha, the learning rate times dw1.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:28.100
b1 gets updated as b1 minus the learning rate times db1.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.500
And similarly for w2 and b2.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.740
And sometimes I use colon equals and sometimes equals as either notation works fine.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.820
And so this would be one iteration of gradient descent.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.260
And then you repeat this some number of times until your parameters look like

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.100
they're converging.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.060
So in previous videos, we talked about how to compute the predictions,

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.340
how to compute the outputs.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.100
And we saw how to do that in a vectorized way as well.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.780
So the key is to know how to compute these partial derivative terms,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.580
the dw1, db1, as well as the derivatives, dw2 and db2.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:09.620
So what I'd like to do is just give you the equations you need in order to compute

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.020
these derivatives.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.660
And I'll defer to the next video, which is an optional video, to go greater into

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.460
depth about how we came up with those formulas.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:25.900
So let me just summarize again the equations for a propagation.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:32.860
So you have z1 equals w1x plus b1.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:38.620
And then a1 equals the activation function in that layer,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.940
applied element-wise of z1.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:50.860
And then z2 equals w2 a1 plus b2.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.260
And then finally, this is all vectorized across your training set, right?

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:00.740
a2 is equal to g2 of z2.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.540
Again, for now, if we assume you're doing binary classification,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:07.060
then this activation function really should be the sigmoid function.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.140
So I'm just throw that in here.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:13.860
So that's the forward propagation or the left to right forward computation for

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:14.900
your neural network.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.820
Next, let's compute the derivatives.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:22.260
So this is the back propagation step.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:31.300
Gonna compute dz2 equals a2 minus the ground truth y.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.060
And just as a reminder, all this is vectorized across examples.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:43.140
So the matrix y is this 1 by m matrix that lists all of your m examples stacked

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.980
horizontally.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:50.900
Then it turns out dw2 is equal to this.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:58.340
In fact, these first three equations are very similar to gradient descent for

00:05:58.340 --> 00:05:59.460
logistic regression.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:08.740
comma x is equals 1, comma keep dms equals true.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:15.540
And just a little detail, this np.sum is a Python NumPy command for

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:21.460
summing across one dimension of a matrix, in this case, summing horizontally.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.580
And what keep dms does is it prevents Python from outputting one of those

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.060
funny rank 1 arrays where the dimensions was n, comma.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:40.100
So by having keep dms equals true, this ensures that Python outputs for db2 a

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.060
vector that is n by 1.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.060
In fact, technically this will be, I guess, n2 by 1.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.060
In this case, it's just a 1 by 1 number, so maybe it doesn't matter.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:54.500
But later on, we'll see when it really matters.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.300
So, so far what we've done is very similar to logistic regression.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:05.620
But now as you compute, to run back propagation, you would compute this.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:16.040
dz2 times g1 prime of z1.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:22.520
So this quantity g1 prime is the derivative of what was the activation function you use

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.960
for the hidden layer.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.120
And for the output layer, I assume that you're doing binary classification

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.320
with the sigmoid function.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.880
So that's already baked into that formula for dz2.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:36.920
And this times is element-wise product.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:43.000
So this here is going to be an n1 by m matrix.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:51.320
And this here, this element-wise derivative thing is also going to be an n1 by m matrix.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:55.160
And so this times there is an element-wise product of two matrices.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.040
Then finally, dw1 is equal to that.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.480
And db1 is equal to this.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:15.400
And p dot sum dz1 axis equals 1.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.200
Keep dims equals true.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:26.280
So whereas previously, the keep dims may be matter less if n2 is equal to 1.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:28.040
So it's just a 1 by 1 thing.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:28.920
It's just a real number.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:36.200
Here, db1 will be a n1 by 1 vector.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.680
And so you want Python, you want np dot sum to output something of this dimension

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.320
rather than a funny rank 1 array of that dimension,

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.720
which could end up messing up some of your later calculations.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:52.920
The other way would be to not have to keep dim parameters,

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.280
but to explicitly call, you know, reshape,

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.600
to reshape the output of np dot sum into this dimension,

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.960
which you would like db to have.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.560
So that was boy propagation in, I guess, four equations

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:13.400
and back propagation in, I guess, six equations.

